>
>> Nine points from divine protection. More is intensely tedious to get.

Purchased protection is easy but limited and once you have enough items
from shops gold just builds and builds.

+4 shirt; 4 points

>> A +4 DSM; 11 points.
>> A pair of +4 boots; 5 points.

6 from iron shoes or high boots.  Not worth enchanting much in the hopes
of magical boot types.

>> A pair of +4 gauntlets; 5 points.

Early leather gloves are guaranteed if you have good pet skills or are
willing to kill the watch.  Late ones are gauranteed from either of the
two castles.  Not worth enchanting much in the hopes of magical glove
types.

>> A +4 helm; 5 points.

6 from a dwarvish iron helm.  Spell casters will prefer an elven
leather helm no matter the one point of AC.  Not worth enchanting much
in the hopes of magical helmet types.

>> A +4 cloak; 5 points.

6 for a robe, 7 for protection as long as you have MR from some other
source.

A +4 shield, 6 points.  Not worth it for #twoweapon users.

> Thanks, that sums it up!
> Although, I see that getting there requires non-trivial amount of
> magic marker charges.

Or a lot or random monster generation and maybe polypiling.

> I have a +0 burnt pair of yugake and +0 very rusty dented pot. I am
> not sure what should be the replacements for those. Gloves of
> dexterity were suggested in my previous post regarding Castle wand
> wishes. And for helm, a +7 elven helm maybe?

Ideal gloves are dexterity or maybe power, but regular leather gloves
are carried by the troops in both castles.  You'll get a replacement
pair in time.  Dwarvish iron helms are plentiful in the early game and
have better AC.

> Zero divine protection, actually. I have never tried that before so
> this will be my first time. I can buy that from priest of Moloch in
> the Valley right?

Right.  At aligned altars (above the Castle) sacrifest protection is
tedious but unlimited and comes with a wide assortment of other
benefits including blessed spellbooks.

